<Layout title="Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings" description="Our normative method for evidence, human-reviewed cross-context mappings, SKOS correspondence, SSSOM provenance and precise links to DDD Context Maps.">
  <h1>Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Connect meanings without erasing their differences. Every mapping needs a clear
    purpose, evidence, a deliberately chosen relationship and a recorded decision.
    Provenance makes that decision inspectable; it does not turn a plausible match
    into an established identity.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Competency questions organise evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Evidence is collected to answer a scoped business question, not simply because a file
    exists. A good competency question identifies the context, expected result and why the
    answer matters. Evidence may support, contradict or leave the question unresolved.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Example question-to-evidence record</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Question</th><td>Which source supports the legal title associated with this property?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Context owner</th><td>Conveyancing, with a Property Data Services source dependency.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Evidence</th><td>Participant scenario, title record, source metadata and PDTF schema comparison.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Candidate impact</th><td>Legal title resource, property relationship and source-evidence relationship.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Open issue</th><td>Whether the source identifies the title assertion or only supplies a document.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Test</th><td>A competency query can retrieve the property, title and attributed source.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="traceability">Traceability from source to version</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="A traceability receipt binds an attributed source to the question it informs, the exact candidate construct, its review and disposition, and the immutable version that includes the decision.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Evidence traceability receipt for a semantic candidate
  accDescr: An attributed source supports a competency question, which links to an exact candidate construct, review record, disposition and immutable candidate version.
  Source["Attributed source"]:::external
  Question["Competency question"]:::user
  Construct["Candidate construct"]:::process
  Review["Review and dissent"]:::user
  Disposition["Recorded disposition"]:::security
  Version["Immutable candidate version"]:::service
  Source --> Question
  Question --> Construct
  Construct --> Review
  Review --> Disposition
  Disposition --> Version
  </Diagram>
  <p>
    Machine extraction is recorded as a derivation, not as the source authority. If
    evidence conflicts, preserve the competing claims for the responsible group to resolve.
    Do not manufacture consensus or collapse a source document, its claim and the
    real-world thing it describes into one resource.
  </p>
  <p>
    Provenance has an appropriate granularity. A whole resource can share one origin
    when its statements form an immutable unit with consistent provenance. If individual
    statements have different origins, attribution or confidence, record that distinction
    at statement level. PROV-O describes sources, activities and attribution; a
    cross-context mapping's decision metadata follows the SSSOM profile below.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mapping">Five qualified mapping meanings</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>These records must not share an unqualified “mapping” label</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Qualified meaning</th><th scope="col">What it connects</th><th scope="col">Standing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Legacy schema–ontology verification</th><td>PDTF schema structures and the schema-derived ontology, including historical RML verification.</td><td>Retained implementation and traceability evidence; not the greenfield domain model.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Legacy JSON-LD context mapping</th><td>Legacy JSON property names and ontology terms for compatibility views.</td><td>Planned or retained migration work; not new semantic authority.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SPDTF cross-context semantic mapping</th><td>Meanings at bounded-context boundaries, with the exact relationship made explicit.</td><td>Category 8: human-reviewed SKOS assertions with SSSOM records under the selected ODRs.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Pack source-item coverage link</th><td>One of 451 required source items and one or more candidate constructs.</td><td>Coverage evidence only; <strong>not RML or R2RML</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Deployment transformation runtime</th><td>Operational representations where a deployment needs a transform.</td><td>Optional implementation service; cannot decide semantic meaning.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    SPDTF retains eight concerns from ODR-0071, including Category 8 cross-domain
    mappings. Category 13 source mappings is excluded from the domain-model scope.
    Keeping historical conversion evidence or linking a requirement to a candidate
    construct does not reinstate an executable RML/R2RML integration model.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cross-context-mappings">Category 8: cross-context mappings</h2>
  <p>
    Category 8 is the bridge between independently governed contexts. It records
    correspondence and strategic relationships while each context owns its domain
    meanings. ODR-0071h defines this boundary; ODR-0087 and ODR-0096 specify SSSOM and
    its statement representation; ODR-0098 governs identity judgments; ODR-0127 connects
    exact term mappings to a strategic Context Map. These selected ODRs are normative
    for our method. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision basis</a>
    records their scope and current amendments alongside the SPDTF programme decisions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep three mechanisms separate. A <a href="/semantic-modelling/context-maps">Context
    Map</a> records relationships between contexts and their responsibilities. The Common boundary
    contains a deliberately small set of shared exchange meanings. A term-level mapping
    keeps two meanings in their own semantic homes and records a reviewed relationship
    between them. A context-map arrow is therefore not, by itself, a mapping assertion.
  </p>
  <div class="callout callout--key">
    <p class="callout__label">Required method and current candidate</p>
    <p>
      SKOS, SSSOM and SEMAPV form the required mapping method. The current Property Pack
      <a href="/development/property-pack/relationships#cross-context">cross-context mapping register</a>
      is empty: no reviewed cross-domain mapping assertions are present. That is an
      implementation and review state, not a deferral of the governing method. A diagram
      of semantic homes and Common-boundary references is not evidence of a populated
      mapping set or implemented strategic Context Map.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="skos-and-sssom">SKOS states the correspondence; SSSOM records its basis</h3>
  <p>
    A reviewed SKOS triple states the relationship between the mapped meanings.
    SSSOM supplies the mapping record, and SEMAPV supplies controlled values for
    <code>mapping_justification</code>. This applies to internal cross-context mappings,
    not only future external-vocabulary exchange. The record preserves the sources,
    versions, justification and date without replacing the asserted SKOS relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/standards#standard-sssom">detailed SSSOM profile</a>
    distinguishes the selected method from current output. The earlier external-only
    deferral in <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0002">OPDA ODR-0002</a> is retained as
    historical evidence, with a dated scope correction for this method.
  </p>
  <p>
    The selected method includes mappings between terms represented as OWL classes.
    It uses SKOS directly on class IRIs for conceptual correspondence, without
    requiring an additional explicit <code>rdf:type skos:Concept</code> assertion.
    SKOS predicates retain their standard semantics, including concept typing that
    can follow under the relevant inference regime; being an OWL class and a SKOS
    concept is not inherently disjoint. This intentional concept view does not
    assert that the class extensions or their instances are identical, and does not
    turn <code>skos:exactMatch</code> into <code>owl:equivalentClass</code>. Do not
    dual-type every class indiscriminately or infer a predicate's applicability merely
    because SSSOM can carry arbitrary predicates.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>The mapping profile separates the statement from its evidence</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">Required meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">SKOS assertion</th><td>Identified endpoints and one deliberately chosen SKOS mapping predicate, retained as an asserted triple.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Statement identity</th><td>A named mapping resource identifies that exact triple through <code>rdf:reifies</code> and an RDF 1.2 triple term.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Source identity</th><td>The subject and object identifier systems, each with a pinned source version.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Decision basis</th><td>Exactly one SEMAPV mapping justification and a mapping date; confidence is optional and never substitutes for justification.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Released set</th><td>A versioned mapping-set identifier, with scope, review disposition and a validation receipt maintained through the governed release process.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The selected ODR-0087 profile uses SSSOM 1.0. Its required source fields are
    <code>sssom:subject_source</code>, <code>sssom:subject_source_version</code>,
    <code>sssom:object_source</code> and <code>sssom:object_source_version</code>,
    together with <code>sssom:mapping_set_id</code>,
    <code>sssom:mapping_justification</code> and <code>sssom:mapping_date</code>.
    A source identifies a vocabulary or identifier system, not merely a context
    label or an IRI prefix guessed from the endpoint's spelling.
  </p>
  <p>
    SSSOM's <code>subject_id</code>, <code>predicate_id</code> and <code>object_id</code>
    are model slots. In the selected canonical RDF representation, the triple term
    supplies those three components; the slot names are not invented RDF predicates.
    Reifying a statement does not assert it: validation must also establish that the
    exact retained SKOS triple exists. OWL annotation-axiom and SSSOM tabular forms
    are derived exports, not separately edited competing records.
  </p>
  <p>
    The active profile excludes <code>sssom:author_id</code>; its removal does
    not remove the need for a recorded human decision. Do not fabricate an agent
    resource to fill a field. Preserve the review and approval record in its proper
    governance home, and use optional confidence, comments and tool details only
    with their defined meanings.
  </p>

  <h3 id="skos-predicates">Choose the SKOS predicate deliberately</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Predicate</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Review warning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:exactMatch</code></th><td>The concepts can be used interchangeably across a wide range of information-retrieval applications. It is a subproperty of <code>skos:closeMatch</code>.</td><td>Symmetric and transitive: a bad decision can propagate through a chain. Review equivalence chains and never use it as a substitute for <code>owl:sameAs</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:closeMatch</code></th><td>The concepts are similar enough to substitute in some information-retrieval applications.</td><td>Symmetric but deliberately not transitive.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:broadMatch</code></th><td>The object concept is broader than the subject concept.</td><td>Direction matters. It is a subproperty of <code>skos:broader</code>, not OWL subclassing, and is not itself transitive.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:narrowMatch</code></th><td>The object concept is narrower than the subject concept.</td><td>It is the inverse of <code>skos:broadMatch</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:relatedMatch</code></th><td>The concepts have a useful symmetric associative relationship without hierarchy or equivalence.</td><td>State the use case; shared wording alone is not evidence.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Predicate combinations also need review. <code>skos:exactMatch</code> is disjoint
    with <code>skos:broadMatch</code> and <code>skos:relatedMatch</code>; symmetry and
    the broad/narrow inverse also make it disjoint with <code>skos:narrowMatch</code>.
    The same pair cannot consistently be both exact and hierarchically or merely
    associatively mapped. See the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/#mapping">SKOS mapping integrity conditions</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="mapping-workflow">From candidate comparison to governed mapping set</h3>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The required mapping workflow keeps machine suggestions advisory. Affected semantic owners and Interoperability review meaning and identity; the resulting SKOS assertion and SSSOM evidence record are validated together before a governed mapping-set release.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Human-governed cross-context mapping workflow
  accDescr: Meanings from two contexts produce an advisory comparison. Human review of definitions, evidence and identity determines a SKOS assertion with a SSSOM record; validation checks both before a mapping-set release.
  MeaningA["Meaning in context A"]:::user
  MeaningB["Meaning in context B"]:::user
  Suggestion["Advisory candidate"]:::external
  Evidence["Definitions, examples and versions"]:::data
  Review["Semantic owners and Interoperability"]:::process
  Predicate["Reviewed mapping predicate"]:::service
  Record["SSSOM record and exact statement"]:::data
  Validate["Semantic and record checks"]:::security
  MappingSet["Governed mapping-set release"]:::external
  MeaningA --> Suggestion
  MeaningB --> Suggestion
  Suggestion -.->|never auto-asserts| Review
  Evidence --> Review
  Review --> Predicate
  Review --> Record
  Predicate --> Validate
  Record --> Validate
  Validate --> MappingSet
  </Diagram>
  <ol>
    <li>Name both semantic owners, exact source versions and the consumer need.</li>
    <li>Let tools suggest candidates from labels, structure or usage, but machines must not assert mappings.</li>
    <li>Compare definitions, examples, counterexamples, identity criteria, logical shape and context.</li>
    <li>Choose reuse, Common-boundary contribution, a qualified predicate or an explicit no-map decision.</li>
    <li>Record the exact statement, pinned sources, SEMAPV justification, date and mapping-set identity in the SSSOM profile. Preserve evidence, dissent and human disposition.</li>
    <li>Validate record completeness and, after recorded human disposition and separate publication authority, promote an immutable mapping-set version. Technical checks do not establish semantic equivalence.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    ODR-0127 then connects a strategic context relationship to the individual mapping
    records applicable at that boundary. A whole mapping set is too imprecise: it may
    contain several context pairs. The join must verify that each mapping's governed
    identifier systems belong to the relationship's endpoint contexts. The direction
    of a SKOS broad/narrow mapping does not determine which context is upstream.
  </p>

  <h2 id="strength">Mapping strength and identity</h2>
  <p>
    ODR-0098 makes identity criterion-based, not similarity-based. A business key
    raises a candidate comparison; it is not proof of conceptual identity. Compare
    identity criteria, rigidity, the unity and boundary of each thing, and the
    evidence supplied by the matching identifiers. A role and its bearer cannot be
    merged merely because they use the same record key.
  </p>
  <p>
    Be conservative at the equivalence tier, but do not refuse useful weaker
    correspondence. A partial correspondence may justify <code>skos:closeMatch</code>
    without <code>skos:exactMatch</code>. Keep relatedness, an explicitly denied
    candidate predicate and a finding of no relationship distinct. A scoped denial
    may use SSSOM's <code>predicate_modifier</code> value <code>Not</code>; absence of
    a mapping is not a recorded denial.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not use <code>owl:sameAs</code> to collapse context-owned concepts, or paired
    subclass links as a workaround for equivalence. The selected method's narrow
    allowance for warranted instance co-reference is a different decision: it needs
    a validated link key and an agreed identity criterion. New evidence must be able
    to reopen a previously accepted or rejected comparison. This is the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/principles">map-over-merge principle</a> in practice.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Choose sharing before mapping only when meaning is genuinely common</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">Preferred treatment</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">A governed external term exactly fits</th><td>Reuse the pinned term-level profile.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Several contexts require one stable exchange meaning</th><td>Contribute a reviewed term to the small common boundary.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Contexts retain different but related meanings</th><td>Keep both semantic homes and record a qualified mapping.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Only a source field corresponds to a candidate construct</th><td>Record a coverage link, not an ontology identity mapping.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="receipt">Evidence receipt</h2>
  <p>
    A candidate receipt identifies source, version, permission, sensitivity, context
    owner, question, proposed change, affected semantic outputs, review record,
    disposition, standards-profile version and validation scope. A PDTF schema link must say
    whether it is evidence, corroboration, compatibility, comparison or supersession.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/validation">Next: validation, review and projections →</a></p>
</Layout>
